  "title": "inheritance polymorphism interfaces abstract classes and access modifiers",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Inheritance reuses and specializes an existing class contract, but it also couples derived behavior to a base implementation. Use it for a genuine substitutable relationship; prefer composition when a type only needs another object's capability.",
    "Virtual dispatch selects an override using the runtime object type, while nonvirtual member lookup is determined by the compile-time type. The `new` modifier hides a base member and does not override it.",
    "An abstract class can share state and implementation while requiring derived types to implement abstract members. An interface describes a capability contract and supports multiple implementation inheritance without sharing instance storage.",
    "Interfaces can provide default implementations and static abstract contracts. The selected feature may require a runtime capability; language syntax, target framework, and runtime support must be considered together.",
    "Access modifiers define which code may use a type or member. The effective accessibility of a member cannot exceed the accessibility of its containing type or exposed signature types.",
    "Polymorphism is valuable when callers depend on a stable abstraction and multiple implementations are interchangeable. Excessive inheritance hierarchies, fragile base classes, and hidden member behavior make changes harder to reason about."
  ],
